StorageGRID & NVMe
StorageGrid is object storage, not block storage, so it doesn't use NVMe/FC or NVMe/TCP directly for client data. However:

StorageGrid Appliances (like SG6060, SGF6024, SG6160) internally use NVMe SSDs to accelerate metadata access and cache.
Newer appliances like SGF6112 are all-NVMe for high-performance workloads.
StorageGrid itself connects to clients via S3/Swift APIs over HTTP/HTTPS (not NVMe).
Key Point: If you're supporting StorageGrid customers, you won't configure NVMe/FC or NVMe/TCP directly—the appliances handle that internally. But you'll see it when supporting E-Series arrays (block storage) or ONTAP systems (block + file).

E-Series & NVMe/TCP/FC
NetApp E-Series systems (EF600, E5700, etc.) are block storage arrays that do support NVMe/FC and NVMe/TCP. Configuration is similar to ONTAP but via SANtricity System Manager instead:

Enable NVMe/FC or NVMe/TCP in Settings → System → Feature Settings → NVMe
Configure host ports and assign IP addresses (for TCP)
Create host groups using host NQN
Map volumes to host groups
Verify via nvme list on the host
Key Takeaways for Support
NVMe/FC = High performance, mission-critical, Fibre Channel-based
NVMe/TCP = Cost-effective, flexible, Ethernet-based
Always check:
Host NQN matches subsystem registration
Namespaces are created and mapped
Networking/zoning is correct
Firmware/driver versions are compatible
StorageGrid doesn't use NVMe/FC/TCP for client access (S3 only)
E-Series and ONTAP systems use both protocols extensively
