NVMe over FC and TCP Reference Cheat Sheet
Overview
NVMe is the protocol for SSD-backed storage. It can run over:

NVMe/FC (Fibre Channel)
NVMe/TCP (Ethernet)
Local PCIe (direct attached)
Common use cases:

ONTAP block storage
E-Series block storage
Host connectivity to array controllers
High-performance SSD workloads
Quick Comparison
Protocol	Transport	Common Hardware	Performance	Typical Use
NVMe/FC	Fibre Channel	FC HBA + FC switch	Very low latency, very high performance	Enterprise SAN
NVMe/TCP	Ethernet	NIC + switch	Lower latency than general TCP workloads, flexible	Cost-effective, IP-based
NVMe local	PCIe	Internal SSD	Lowest latency	Local NVMe drives
Key Terms
NQN: NVMe Qualified Name
Namespace: Logical storage unit exposed to the host
Subsystem: NVMe target or controller group
WWPN: Fibre Channel worldwide port name
Transport: FC or TCP
LIF: Logical interface (ONTAP)
Host: Server connecting to storage
NVMe/FC Reference
Host-side commands (Linux)
bash
# Show host NQN
cat /etc/nvme/hostnqn

# Discover NVMe/FC target
nvme discover -t fc -a <target_ip_or_fabric_info>

# Connect to subsystem
nvme connect -t fc -n <subsystem_nqn> --traddr=<target_wwpn>

# List connected NVMe devices
nvme list

# Show subsystem topology
nvme list-subsys

# View NVMe device details
nvme id-ns /dev/nvme0n1
nvme id-ctrl /dev/nvme0n1
NVMe/FC diagnostics
bash
# View NVMe devices and paths
nvme list-subsys -o json
nvme list

# Show kernel NVMe messages
dmesg | grep -i nvme

# Linux logs
journalctl -k | grep -i nvme
NetApp ONTAP commands
bash
# Show NVMe status
vserver nvme show

# Show NVMe interfaces
network interface show -data-protocol nvme-fc

# Show subsystems
vserver nvme subsystems show

# Show active connections
vserver nvme connections show

# Show namespaces
vserver nvme namespace show

# Show namespace mappings
vserver nvme subsystem map show

# Review errors
event log show -message-name *nvme* -severity error
Common NVMe/FC issues
Host cannot see target
Zone not configured
Host NQN not added to subsystem
Namespace not mapped
FC ports down or mis-zoned
Switch CRC or buffer credit errors
FC-specific checks
bash
# Show FC HBA info
lspci | grep -i fibre
lspci | grep -i broadcom
lspci | grep -i emulex

# Check host HBA status
cat /sys/class/fc_host/host*/port_name
cat /sys/class/fc_host/host*/port_state
NVMe/TCP Reference
Host-side commands (Linux)
bash
# Show host NQN
cat /etc/nvme/hostnqn

# Discover NVMe/TCP targets
nvme discover -t tcp -a <target_ip> -s 4420

# Connect to target
nvme connect -t tcp -a <target_ip> -s 4420 -n <subsystem_nqn>

# List connected devices
nvme list

# Show subsystem information
nvme list-subsys

# Disconnect from target
nvme disconnect -n <subsystem_nqn>
TCP diagnostics
bash
# Ping target LIF
ping <target_ip>

# Verify port 4420 is reachable
nc -zv <target_ip> 4420

# Check MTU
ip link show

# Set MTU to jumbo frames
ip link set dev <interface> mtu 9000
NetApp ONTAP commands
bash
# Show NVMe/TCP LIFs
network interface show -data-protocol nvme-tcp

# Show NVMe status
vserver nvme show

# Show NVMe subsystems
vserver nvme subsystems show

# Show active NVMe TCP connections
vserver nvme connections show

# Show namespaces
vserver nvme namespace show

# Verify network ports
network port show
Common NVMe/TCP issues
Port 4420 blocked by firewall
LIF not created or not online
MTU mismatch
VLAN issue
Network path congestion
IP routing or DNS problems
Host NQN not allowed in subsystem
NVMe/TCP troubleshooting basics
bash
# Check target reachability
ping <target_ip>
nc -zv <target_ip> 4420

# Check interface status
